/ActualText (6.2 Why Containerization Matters)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1008 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Containerization offers several benefits:)
/E (Containerization offers several benefits:)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1009 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2059 0 R
/ActualText (Portability: Containers encapsulate everything your application needs to run, eliminating the "it works on my machine" problem.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1010 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2060 0 R
/ActualText (Efficiency: Containers are lightweight and share the host operating system's kernel, making them more efficient than traditional virtual machines.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1011 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2061 0 R
/ActualText (Scalability: Containers can be easily scaled up or down to handle varying workloads, ensuring optimal performance and resource utilization.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1012 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2062 0 R
/ActualText (Isolation: Containers provide isolation between applications, preventing conflicts and ensuring that one application's failure doesn't impact others.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
